<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-5-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Logarithmic Functions</title>

<assemblage component="instructor"><p>In Chapter 4 we treated the logarithm as an operation; taking a log was the inverse operation for raising to a power, just as taking a square root is the inverse operation for squaring. Now we investigate the log function as the inverse function for the exponential function. We also solve logarithmic equations using two steps: we use the log properties to combine the logs into one log, then convert to exponential form.</p>

<p>Applications of log functions are not nearly as common as applications of exponential functions, so most students will find that thinking of logarithms as an operation is more useful than using a log function, but the added practice provided by this Lesson will probably be helpful.</p>

<p>GOALS</p>
<p><ul>
    <li><p>Evaluate logarithmic functions</p></li>
    <li><p>Graph logarithmic functions</p></li>
    <li><p>Solve logarithmic equations</p></li>
</ul></p>
</assemblage>

<activity xml:id="act-5-2-1"><title> Making a Choice</title>

<introduction><p>Hick's Law says that the time, <m>T</m>, required for a person to make a choice from a list or menu of <m>n</m> items is proportional to <m>\log_2 (n+1)</m>. A classic study measured the amount of time shoppers took to choose a flavor of jam from a display of 6 flavors and from a display of 24 flavors.  Hick's Law is used by restaurants and web-designers in making menus, and by survey companies in designing multiple-choice questionaires.</p></introduction>

    <aside component="instructor"><p>Hick's Law is an interesting logarithmic model from psychology, along with the Weber-Fechner Law. Why logs base 2?  Psychologists hypothesize that the mind divides a list successively into halves.</p></aside>

<p><ol marker="a.">
    <li><p>Graciela is choosing an AirBnB for her next vacation. If there are <m>n</m> choices available, it will take her <m>T</m> minutes to make her choice, where
        <md>T=5\log_2 (n+1)</md></p>

    <p>Complete the table and make a graph.</p>

        <aside component="instructor"><p>1(a) Although <m>n=0</m> does not make sense for the application, point out that we can add that point to complete the graph.</p></aside>

        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>n</m></cell>
                    <cell>3</cell>
                    <cell>7</cell>
                    <cell>15</cell>
                    <cell>31</cell></row>
                <row><cell><m>T</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
            </tabular>

            <image source="act-10-1-7.png" width="60%"><description>grid 0 to 36 by 0 to 36</description></image></li>

    <li><p>If Graciela has 30 minutes to spend choosing an AirBnB, how many can she consider?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="act-5-2-2"><title>A Log Function</title>
<introduction><p>In this activity we compare the graphs of <m>y=\sqrt{x}</m> and <m>y=\log_2{x}</m>.</p></introduction>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>The goal of Activity 2 is to demonstrate that logarithmic functions grow very slowly, just as exponential functions grow rapidly.</p></aside>

<p><ol marker="a.">
<li><p>Use your calculator to complete the table. Use the formula <m>~\log_2{x} = \dfrac{\log{x}}{\log{2}}</m> when needed to evaluate <m>\log_2{x}</m>. Round values to tenths.</p>

<sidebyside widths="40% 55%" valigns="middle middle">
        <tabular top="minor" halign="center" right="minor" left="minor" bottom="minor">              
                <row bottom="minor">
                    <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\sqrt{x}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\log_2{x}</m></cell></row>
                <row>
                    <cell>0</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>1</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>2</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>4</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
                <row><cell>6</cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell>
                    <cell><m>\hphantom{00000}</m></cell></row>
