            </tabular>
        <image source="act-10-1-8.png"></image><description>grid 0 to 20 by -2 to 5</description></sidebyside></li>

<li><p>Plot both functions on the grid.</p>

        <aside component="instructor"><p>2(b) The graphs are very close; students will have to be precise in their work. After they have finished their graphs, it might be instructive to display a large version using Desmos or some other graphing facility.</p></aside></li>

<li><p>At which two points do the graphs intersect?</p>
    <p>Which graph has larger function values in the long run?</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<activity xml:id="act-5-2-3"><title>Logarithmic Equations</title>

<p><ol marker="a.">
<li><p>What is a log equation?</p>

            <aside component="instructor"><p>3(b) We solve simple log equations by converting to exponential form. You may need to review the equivalence with some numerical examples.</p></aside></li>

<li><p>Follow the steps to solve the equation <m>~\log_2 {(3x-1)} = 5</m></p>
    <p><m>\blert{\text{Convert to exponential form.}}</m></p>
    <p><m>\blert{\text{Solve the new equation.}}</m></p>
    <p><m>\blert{\text{Check the solution.}}</m></p></li>

<li><p> To solve more complicated log equations, we first use the log properties to combine any expressions 
containing logs into a single logarithm.</p>

           <aside component="instructor"><p>3(c) Stress that the first step is to combine the logs into one log, using the log properties.</p></aside>

<p>Follow the steps to solve <m>~~\log_4 {(x+8)} + \log_4 {(x+2)} = 2</m></p>
  <p><m>\blert{\text{Use log properties to write the left side as a single log.}}</m></p> 
  <p><m>\blert{\text{Convert the equation to exponential form.}}</m></p>
  <p><m>\blert{\text{Solve the new equation.}}</m></p>
  <p><m>\blert{\text{Don't forget to check for extraneous solutions!}}</m></p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>If you double the number of choices under Hick's Law in <xref ref="act-5-2-1" text="custom">Activity 1</xref>, approximately how much more time will you need to make your choice?</p></li>
    <li><p>Suppose you don't have a calculator handy. How can you estimate the value of <m>\log_2 {50}</m>?</p></li>
    <li><p>How do we check for extraneous solutions when solving a logarithmic equation?</p></li>
    <li><p>How might you use the properties of logarithms when solving a logarithmic equation?</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Explain why we cannot take the log of a negative number.</p></li>
    <li><p>Why does the function <m>f(x) = \log{x}</m>  grow so slowly?</p></li>
    <li><p>Compare the graphs of <m>f(x) = \log{\dfrac{1}{x}}</m>  and <m>g(x) = -\log{x}</m>, and explain.</p></li>
    <li><p>What is an extraneous solution?</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>What is the first step in solving  <m>~\log {x} + \log{x-1} = 2</m>?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><p><m>\log{2x-1}=2</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>\log{x} = 2 - \log{x-1}</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>\log{x(x-1)} = 2</m></p></li>
        <li><p><m>x + x-1 = 2^x</m></p></li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>The domain of the function <m>\log_3{x}</m> is</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li>all real numbers</li>
        <li>all multiples of 3</li>
        <li>all non-negative numbers</li>
        <li>all positive numbers</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>What is the <m>y</m>-intercept of the graph of <m>f(x) = \log_5{x}</m> ?</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>(0,1)</m></li>
        <li><m>(1,0)</m></li>
        <li><m>(0,5)</m></li>
        <li>There is none.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>If <m>F(x) = \log_2{x}</m>, then <m>F^{-1}(x)</m> is</p>
        <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{\log_2{x}}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=2^x</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{2x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\log{\dfrac{1}{2x}}</m></li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>